(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the architecture and operational principles of the Domain Name System \(DNS\), detailing root servers, TLD servers, and authoritative servers.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The Domain Name System \(DNS\) is a distributed, hierarchical database system that maps) Tj
T*
(   human-readable domain names \(such as www.example.com\) to machine-routable IP addresses \(such as) Tj
T*
(   93.184.216.34\):) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   DNS Architectural Hierarchy:) Tj
T*
(   1. DNS Root Servers: At the apex of the DNS tree sits the root zone \(denoted by a trailing dot) Tj
T*
(   '.'\). There are 13 logical root server addresses \(A through M, operated by entities like ICANN,) Tj
T*
(   NASA, Verisign\), replicated globally across hundreds of Anycast locations. Root servers direct) Tj
T*
(   queries to Top-Level Domain \(TLD\) servers.) Tj
T*
(   2. Top-Level Domain \(TLD\) Servers: Responsible for generic TLDs \(gTLDs like .com, .org, .edu\)) Tj
T*
(   and country-code TLDs \(ccTLDs like .in, .uk, .de\). TLD servers maintain pointers to) Tj
T*
(   authoritative nameservers for domains registered under them.) Tj
T*
(   3. Authoritative Name Servers: Maintained by domain owners or hosting providers. An) Tj
T*
(   authoritative nameserver holds the actual Resource Records \(RRs\) for a specific domain and) Tj
T*
(   provides definitive answers for queries about its hosts.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Resolution Process:) Tj
T*
(   When an end-user queries a domain, their local recursive resolver checks its cache. If missing,) Tj
T*
(   it queries a Root server, follows the referral to the TLD server, follows that referral to the) Tj
T*
(   Authoritative server, and finally returns the resolved IP address to the user's browser,) Tj
T*
(   caching the result based on the record's Time-To-Live \(TTL\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Compare Iterative DNS Queries and Recursive DNS Queries, illustrating the exact packet exchange sequence with an example.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   DNS resolution utilizes two distinct querying paradigms:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Recursive Query:) Tj
T*
(   - In a recursive query, the querying client puts the entire burden of resolution onto the) Tj
T*
(   contacted server.) Tj
T*
(   - The server must return either the exact answer \(IP address\) or an explicit error message) Tj
T*
(   \('Domain Not Found'\). It cannot pass off a referral.) Tj
T*
(   - Standard usage: End-user workstations and laptops issue recursive queries to their Local DNS) Tj
T*
(   Resolvers \(e.g., corporate DNS or public resolvers like 8.8.8.8\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Iterative Query:) Tj
T*
(   - In an iterative query, if the queried server does not possess the exact authoritative record) Tj
T*
(   in its local zone or cache, it returns a referral: 'I don't know the answer, but you should ask) Tj
T*
(   this next nameserver.') Tj
T*
(   - The querying resolver is responsible for following successive referrals.) Tj
T*
(   - Standard usage: Local DNS resolvers make iterative queries to Root servers, TLD servers, and) Tj
T*
(   authoritative nameservers to resolve names without overburdening upper-tier root) Tj
T*
(   infrastructure.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
