(>> Solution to Question 3:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the primary DNS Resource Record \(RR\) types: A, AAAA, CNAME, MX, NS, PTR, SOA, and TXT.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   DNS databases store configuration entries formatted as Resource Records \(RRs\), each defined by) Tj
T*
(   \(Name, Value, Type, TTL\):) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Type A \(IPv4 Address\): Maps a hostname to a 32-bit IPv4 address \(e.g., example.com ->) Tj
T*
(   192.0.2.1\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Type AAAA \(IPv6 Address\): Maps a hostname to a 128-bit IPv6 address \(e.g., example.com ->) Tj
T*
(   2606:2800:220:1:248:1893:25c8:1946\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Type CNAME \(Canonical Name\): Defines an alias pointing one domain name to another canonical) Tj
T*
(   hostname \(e.g., www.example.com -> example.com\).) Tj
T*
(   4. Type MX \(Mail Exchange\): Specifies the mail server responsible for receiving emails for the) Tj
T*
(   domain, along with a priority metric \(e.g., example.com -> mail.example.com, priority 10\).) Tj
T*
(   5. Type NS \(Name Server\): Delegates a DNS zone to use a specific authoritative nameserver) Tj
T*
(   \(e.g., example.com -> ns1.example.com\).) Tj
T*
(   6. Type PTR \(Pointer\): Maps an IP address to a hostname for Reverse DNS \(rDNS\) lookups under) Tj
T*
(   the in-addr.arpa domain.) Tj
T*
(   7. Type SOA \(Start of Authority\): Contains administrative parameters for the zone, including) Tj
T*
(   primary master nameserver, administrator email, serial number, refresh intervals, and default) Tj
T*
(   TTL.) Tj
T*
(   8. Type TXT \(Text\): Holds arbitrary human/machine-readable text, critically used for domain) Tj
T*
(   verification and email anti-spoofing standards \(SPF, DKIM, DMARC\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 4:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the architecture of File Transfer Protocol \(FTP\), distinguishing between Active Mode and Passive Mode \(PASV\) and their firewall implications.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   FTP \(RFC 959\) is a client-server protocol designed for robust file transfer. It is distinctive) Tj
T*
(   in utilizing two separate TCP connections:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Control Connection \(Port 21\): Established first by the client. Remains open throughout the) Tj
T*
(   session to transmit ASCII commands \(USER, PASS, LIST, RETR, STOR\) and receive 3-digit server) Tj
T*
(   status responses.) Tj
T*
(   2. Data Connection: Created dynamically on-demand whenever a file or directory listing is) Tj
T*
(   transferred, and closed immediately after completion.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Active Mode vs Passive Mode:) Tj
T*
(   - Active Mode: The client sends a PORT command specifying an ephemeral port on the client. The) Tj
T*
(   server initiates an outbound connection from Server Port 20 to the client's port. Problem:) Tj
T*
(   Client-side firewalls and NAT gateways block unsolicited inbound connections from the server,) Tj
T*
(   causing active transfers to fail.) Tj
T*
(   - Passive Mode \(PASV\): The client sends PASV command. The server opens an ephemeral high port) Tj
T*
(   \(e.g., 50001\) and informs the client. The client initiates the outbound connection to the) Tj
T*
(   server's data port. Because the connection is outbound from the client, client-side firewalls) Tj
T*
(   and NAT permit the connection effortlessly, making PASV the modern standard.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 5:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the end-to-end architecture of Electronic Mail systems, explaining the roles of MUA, MTA, MDA, SMTP, POP3, and IMAP.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Internet email architecture separates message dispatch from message retrieval across discrete) Tj
T*
