BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the evolution of Hypertext Transfer Protocol from HTTP/1.0, to HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The HTTP protocol has evolved dramatically to meet modern web performance requirements:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. HTTP/1.0 \(1996\): Non-persistent connections. Every single web resource \(HTML, CSS, image\)) Tj
T*
(   required a separate TCP 3-way handshake and teardown, incurring massive latency.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. HTTP/1.1 \(1997, RFC 2616\): Introduced Persistent Connections \(Keep-Alive\) by default,) Tj
T*
(   allowing multiple requests over a single TCP connection. Added pipelining, chunked transfer) Tj
T*
(   encoding, and virtual hosting \(Host header\). However, suffered from HTTP Head-of-Line \(HoL\)) Tj
T*
(   blocking \(requests had to be processed sequentially\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. HTTP/2 \(2015, RFC 7540\): Replaced text formatting with a Binary Framing Layer. Introduced) Tj
T*
(   full bidirectional multiplexing: dozens of requests/responses share a single TCP connection as) Tj
T*
(   independent frames, eliminating HTTP HoL blocking. Added HPACK header compression and Server) Tj
T*
(   Push.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. HTTP/3 \(2022, RFC 9114\): Shifted underlying transport from TCP to QUIC \(built over UDP\).) Tj
T*
(   Eliminates Transport-Layer HoL blocking \(a dropped packet only pauses that specific stream, not) Tj
T*
(   the whole connection\), integrates TLS 1.3 natively, and supports seamless connection migration) Tj
T*
(   across IP networks.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
