(   [Problem Statement]: Compare Symmetric-Key and Asymmetric-Key Cryptography, analyzing security, key distribution, computational overhead, and typical hybrid usage.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Cryptography bifurcates into two complementary models:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Symmetric-Key Cryptography \(e.g., AES, ChaCha20\):) Tj
T*
(   - Uses the same secret key for both encryption and decryption.) Tj
T*
(   - Pros: Extremely fast, computationally lightweight, highly optimized in modern CPU hardware) Tj
T*
(   instructions \(AES-NI\).) Tj
T*
(   - Cons: Key distribution problem \(how to securely share secret key across insecure network\) and) Tj
T*
(   key management scalability \(requires n*\(n-1\)/2 keys for n users\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Asymmetric-Key Cryptography \(e.g., RSA, ECC, Diffie-Hellman\):) Tj
T*
(   - Uses mathematically linked key pairs: Public Key \(openly published\) and Private Key \(secret\).) Tj
T*
(   - Pros: Solves key distribution \(anyone can encrypt with public key; only private key holder) Tj
T*
(   can decrypt\) and enables digital signatures.) Tj
T*
(   - Cons: Computationally expensive, orders of magnitude slower than symmetric ciphers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Practical Hybrid Cryptosystems \(e.g., TLS, SSH, PGP\):) Tj
T*
(   Modern systems combine both: Asymmetric cryptography is used during handshake to authenticate) Tj
T*
(   parties and exchange a short-lived random 'session key'. The high-volume bulk application data) Tj
T*
(   is then encrypted using lightning-fast Symmetric cryptography with that session key.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 11:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the mathematical foundations of the RSA public-key cryptosystem: key generation, encryption, decryption, and security basis.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   RSA \(Rivest-Shamir-Adleman\) is an asymmetric algorithm based on the difficulty of prime) Tj
T*
(   factorization:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Key Generation Procedure:) Tj
T*
(   - Select two distinct, large prime numbers p and q.) Tj
T*
(   - Compute modulus n = p * q. Modulus length defines key size \(e.g., 2048 or 4096 bits\).) Tj
T*
(   - Compute Euler's totient function: phi\(n\) = \(p - 1\) * \(q - 1\).) Tj
T*
(   - Choose an integer e \(public exponent\) such that 1 < e < phi\(n\) and gcd\(e, phi\(n\)\) = 1) Tj
T*
(   \(commonly e = 65537\).) Tj
T*
(   - Compute private exponent d such that \(d * e\) mod phi\(n\) = 1 \(using the Extended Euclidean) Tj
T*
(   Algorithm\).) Tj
T*
(   - Public Key: \(e, n\); Private Key: \(d, n\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Encryption and Decryption:) Tj
T*
(   - To encrypt plaintext message m \(where 0 <= m < n\): Ciphertext c = \(m^e\) mod n.) Tj
T*
(   - To decrypt ciphertext c: Plaintext m = \(c^d\) mod n.) Tj
T*
ET

