(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Diffie-Hellman Key Exchange protocol, illustrating the mathematical steps and analyzing its susceptibility to Man-in-the-Middle attacks.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Diffie-Hellman \(DH\) enables two parties to securely generate a shared secret key over an) Tj
T*
(   unsecure public channel without transmitting the key itself:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Protocol Steps:) Tj
T*
(   1. Public Parameters: Alice and Bob publicly agree on a large prime modulus p and a primitive) Tj
T*
(   root generator g.) Tj
T*
(   2. Private Secrets: Alice chooses a random secret private integer a; Bob chooses a random) Tj
T*
(   secret private integer b.) Tj
T*
(   3. Public Key Exchange:) Tj
T*
(   - Alice calculates public value A = \(g^a\) mod p and sends A to Bob.) Tj
T*
(   - Bob calculates public value B = \(g^b\) mod p and sends B to Alice.) Tj
T*
(   4. Computing Shared Secret:) Tj
T*
(   - Alice calculates shared key K = \(B^a\) mod p = \(\(g^b\)^a\) mod p = g^\(ab\) mod p.) Tj
T*
(   - Bob calculates shared key K = \(A^b\) mod p = \(\(g^a\)^b\) mod p = g^\(ab\) mod p.) Tj
T*
(   Both now share key K. An eavesdropper sees \(p, g, A, B\) but cannot compute K without solving) Tj
T*
(   the discrete logarithm problem.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Man-in-the-Middle \(MitM\) Vulnerability:) Tj
T*
(   Unauthenticated DH does not authenticate endpoint identities. An attacker Eve sitting between) Tj
T*
(   Alice and Bob can intercept A, establish DH key K1 with Alice, establish separate DH key K2) Tj
T*
(   with Bob, and decrypt/re-encrypt all traffic transparently. Mitigation: Endpoints authenticate) Tj
T*
(   DH exchanges using Digital Signatures and X.509 certificates \(e.g., in TLS\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 13:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Cryptographic Hash Functions, detailing properties, Merkle-Damgard construction, and comparison of MD5, SHA-1, SHA-2, and SHA-3.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   A cryptographic hash function H maps an arbitrary-length message to a fixed-length bit string) Tj
T*
(   \(digest\):) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Essential Security Properties:) Tj
T*
(   1. Deterministic: The same message always produces the exact same hash output.) Tj
T*
(   2. Pre-image Resistance \(One-Way\): Given digest h, it is computationally infeasible to find) Tj
T*
(   message m such that H\(m\) = h.) Tj
T*
(   3. Second Pre-image Resistance \(Weak Collision Resistance\): Given message m1, it is infeasible) Tj
T*
(   to find a distinct m2 such that H\(m1\) = H\(m2\).) Tj
T*
(   4. Collision Resistance \(Strong Collision Resistance\): It is computationally infeasible to find) Tj
T*
(   ANY pair of distinct messages \(m1, m2\) such that H\(m1\) = H\(m2\).) Tj
T*
(   5. Avalanche Effect: Flipping a single bit in input flips approximately 50% of the output bits) Tj
T*
(   randomly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Algorithm Evolution:) Tj
T*
