(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze common Web Application security vulnerabilities from the OWASP Top 10: SQL Injection, Cross-Site Scripting \(XSS\), and Cross-Site Request Forgery \(CSRF\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Web applications are susceptible to application-layer attacks that bypass perimeter network) Tj
T*
(   firewalls:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. SQL Injection \(SQLi\):) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism: Attacker inputs malicious SQL fragments into unvalidated input fields \(e.g., ' OR) Tj
T*
(   '1'='1\), altering the database query structure.) Tj
T*
(   - Impact: Unauthorized database extraction, data modification, or administrative privilege) Tj
T*
(   takeover.) Tj
T*
(   - Mitigation: Use Parameterized Queries \(Prepared Statements\) and Object-Relational Mappers) Tj
T*
(   \(ORMs\); never concatenate user input into SQL strings.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Cross-Site Scripting \(XSS\):) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism: Attacker injects malicious client-side JavaScript into web applications, which is) Tj
T*
(   rendered and executed in victims' browsers \(Stored, Reflected, or DOM-based\).) Tj
T*
(   - Impact: Stealing session cookies, session hijacking, defacement, or credential phishing.) Tj
T*
(   - Mitigation: Context-aware output encoding, robust input sanitization, and strict Content) Tj
T*
(   Security Policy \(CSP\) headers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Cross-Site Request Forgery \(CSRF\):) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism: Exploits browser's automatic inclusion of session cookies to trick authenticated) Tj
T*
(   users into submitting unwanted commands to a web server.) Tj
T*
(   - Mitigation: Anti-CSRF Synchronizer Tokens, re-authentication for critical transactions, and) Tj
T*
(   SameSite=Strict cookie attributes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
