(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the mechanics of a Distributed Denial of Service \(DDoS\) SYN Flood and describe how SYN Cookies provide stateless defense.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   SYN Flood Attack Mechanics:) Tj
T*
(   1. The attacker coordinates thousands of botnet nodes to transmit TCP SYN packets to target) Tj
T*
(   server port 80/443 with spoofed, non-existent source IP addresses.) Tj
T*
(   2. The server allocates a Transmission Control Block \(TCB\) in kernel memory for each SYN and) Tj
T*
(   returns SYN+ACK.) Tj
T*
(   3. Because source IPs are fake, no third ACK arrives. Server buffers stay allocated in SYN_RECV) Tj
T*
(   state until timeout.) Tj
T*
(   4. Result: The listen backlog table fills up completely, causing the OS to drop new incoming) Tj
T*
(   connection requests from legitimate clients.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   SYN Cookie Defense:) Tj
T*
(   - When backlog fills, server stops allocating memory for incoming SYNs.) Tj
T*
(   - It constructs its Initial Sequence Number \(ISN\) as a cryptographic cookie: ISN =) Tj
T*
(   Hash\(Client_IP, Client_Port, Server_Secret, Timestamp\) + \(MSS_Index\).) Tj
T*
(   - If client is legitimate, it returns ACK with ack = ISN + 1.) Tj
T*
(   - Server subtracts 1, recomputes the cryptographic hash using its secret, and only allocates a) Tj
T*
(   TCB if the token matches, defeating the memory exhaustion exploit.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 23:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the structure of X.509 certificates and how Certificate Transparency \(CT\) logs detect rogue certificates.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Structure of X.509 Certificates:) Tj
T*
(   X.509 certificates encapsulate cryptographic public keys with verified organizational identity) Tj
T*
(   attributes, digitally signed by a Certificate Authority \(CA\) to establish trust chains.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Certificate Transparency \(CT, RFC 6962\):) Tj
T*
(   1. The Problem: Rogue or compromised CAs have historically issued unauthorized certificates for) Tj
T*
(   prominent domains \(e.g., DigiNotar hack issuing rogue *.google.com certs\), allowing undetected) Tj
T*
(   man-in-the-middle attacks.) Tj
T*
(   2. CT Solution: An open, append-only, publicly auditable framework of Merkle Tree log servers.) Tj
T*
(   Every CA must submit issued certificates to public CT logs before browsers accept them.) Tj
T*
(   3. Signed Certificate Timestamp \(SCT\): The CT log returns an SCT proof, embedded in the) Tj
T*
(   certificate. Browsers reject any certificate lacking valid SCT proofs.) Tj
T*
(   4. Domain Monitoring: Domain owners continuously monitor CT logs for unauthorized certificates) Tj
T*
(   issued for their domains, enabling immediate detection and revocation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
