BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Discuss the operating modes of symmetric block ciphers: ECB, CBC, CFB, OFB, and CTR, comparing security and parallelism.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Block ciphers operate on fixed-size blocks \(e.g., 128 bits for AES\). Modes of operation dictate) Tj
T*
(   how sequential blocks are processed:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Electronic Codebook \(ECB\): Encrypts each block independently. Insecure: identical plaintexts) Tj
T*
(   produce identical ciphertexts, exposing structural patterns. No parallelism issue, but) Tj
T*
(   cryptographically broken.) Tj
T*
(   2. Cipher Block Chaining \(CBC\): XORs each plaintext block with previous ciphertext block before) Tj
T*
(   encryption. Requires an IV. Highly secure against pattern analysis, but sequential encryption) Tj
T*
(   prevents multi-core parallelization; single bit errors propagate.) Tj
T*
(   3. Cipher Feedback \(CFB\) & Output Feedback \(OFB\): Turn block ciphers into stream ciphers by) Tj
T*
(   encrypting previous ciphertext or previous keystream blocks.) Tj
T*
(   4. Counter Mode \(CTR\): Encrypts a succession of incrementing counters \(Nonce + Counter\) to) Tj
T*
(   generate a keystream, XORed with plaintext. Highly secure, supports random access, and allows) Tj
T*
(   massive parallel processing on multi-core processors.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 26:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the architecture and implementation of Demilitarized Zones \(DMZ\) in enterprise network security.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   A Demilitarized Zone \(DMZ\) is a dedicated subnetwork positioned between an untrusted public) Tj
T*
(   network \(Internet\) and a trusted internal corporate network \(LAN\):) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Architecture & Topologies:) Tj
T*
(   1. Dual-Homed \(Three-Legged\) Firewall: A single firewall with three interfaces: External) Tj
T*
(   \(Internet\), DMZ \(Public Services\), and Internal \(LAN\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Two-Firewall \(Back-to-Back\) DMZ: An outer perimeter firewall faces the Internet and connects) Tj
T*
(   to DMZ; an inner firewall isolates DMZ from Internal LAN. Employs different firewall vendors to) Tj
T*
(   prevent single-vulnerability bypasses.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Access Control Policies:) Tj
T*
(   - Inbound traffic from Internet is permitted ONLY to DMZ servers on specific application ports) Tj
T*
(   \(HTTP/HTTPS to Web, SMTP to Mail\).) Tj
T*
