(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the principles and operational challenges of Network Address Translation \(NAT\) and its impact on peer-to-peer protocols.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   NAT \(RFC 3022\) translates private IPv4 addresses \(RFC 1918\) to globally routable public IPs,) Tj
T*
(   mitigating IPv4 depletion:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Network Address Port Translation \(NAPT / PAT\):) Tj
T*
(   - Maps thousands of internal private IP:port socket pairs to a single external public IP by) Tj
T*
(   rewriting transport source port numbers in outgoing packet headers and tracking mappings in a) Tj
T*
(   state table.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Impact on Peer-to-Peer \(P2P\) Systems:) Tj
T*
(   - Inbound Connection Blocking: A host behind NAT cannot accept unsolicited inbound connections) Tj
T*
(   from outside peers because no NAT state entry exists.) Tj
T*
(   - P2P Traversal Techniques:) Tj
T*
(   1. STUN \(Session Traversal Utilities for NAT\): Allows a client to discover its public mapped IP) Tj
T*
(   and port.) Tj
T*
(   2. TURN \(Traversal Using Relays around NAT\): Relays media streams through a public third-party) Tj
T*
(   server when symmetric NAT blocks direct peer connections.) Tj
T*
(   3. ICE \(Interactive Connectivity Establishment\): Standardized framework combining STUN and TURN) Tj
T*
(   to establish the optimal direct path between WebRTC peers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
