/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Zero Trust Architecture \(ZTA\) paradigm \(NIST SP 800-207\) and contrast it with traditional perimeter security models.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Traditional enterprise network security relied on the 'Castle-and-Moat' perimeter model: once) Tj
T*
(   inside the corporate firewall or VPN, users and devices were implicitly trusted with broad) Tj
T*
(   access.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The Zero Trust Philosophy:) Tj
T*
(   'Never Trust, Always Verify.' Zero Trust assumes that network perimeters are permeable and) Tj
T*
(   malicious actors may already reside inside the internal network.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Core Pillars of NIST SP 800-207:) Tj
T*
(   1. Continuous Authentication and Authorization: Access requests are dynamically evaluated on a) Tj
T*
(   per-session basis using identity, device posture, location, and behavioral context.) Tj
T*
(   2. Principle of Least Privilege: Users are granted minimal granular access strictly necessary) Tj
T*
(   for their specific tasks \(Micro-segmentation\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Explicit Verification: Every transaction, API call, and data access is cryptographically) Tj
T*
(   verified and authenticated.) Tj
T*
(   4. End-to-End Encryption: All communications are encrypted in transit and at rest, even across) Tj
T*
(   internal LAN switches.) Tj
T*
(   5. Comprehensive Telemetry: Rigorous logging, auditing, and real-time AI security analytics) Tj
T*
(   across all endpoints, networks, and cloud workloads.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
